          ":question/id": "b538b794-53e2-55d6-941e-f772138f1dd6",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-72621",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/6e/6e68ede9db2528ea607fc6fd63f0ec4ca1c1378982239913ef49153e1cf5b609.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(3,-3)$ and the point $X(-1,2)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(-1-3)}^{2}+{(2-(-3))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{41}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & =(\\sqrt{41})^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =41\\end{aligned}\n$$"
        }
      ],
      ":knowledge-point/title": "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given the Center and a Point on the Circle"
